use std
into grow.amni

Namespace grow; imports math, vec, os, dictionary. Builds organic triangle meshes into module arrays. All geometry math is done in `real` with `double3` vectors; the mesh arrays store `f32`. Module vars: `P`, `N` and `C`, growable f32 arrays (x, y, z of each vertex's position, unit normal and linear RGB color), `I`, a growable int array (three vertex ids per triangle, counter-clockwise seen from outside), `S`, a growable real array (points written by `phyllo`, `scatter` and `on_surface`), `FORKS`, a growable real array (two values per tree fork: the parent's end radius and the sum of its children's squared start radii), `st u64` (random state) and `nseed u64` (noise seed). Module constants: `GOLDEN real = 2.39996322972865332` (the golden angle in radians) and `PI2 = 6.28318530717958648`.

`clear()` empties P, N, C, I, S and FORKS. `vcount() int` is the number of vertices and `tcount() int` the number of triangles.

`seed(s int)` sets `st` to `s` (as u64) XOR `0x9E3779B97F4A7C15`, draws one `rnd()`, then sets `nseed` to the next raw 64-bit draw. `next() u64` is splitmix64: add `0x9E3779B97F4A7C15` to `st`, then z = st; z = (z XOR z shifted right 30) times `0xBF58476D1CE4E5B9`; z = (z XOR z shifted right 27) times `0x94D049BB133111EB`; return z XOR z shifted right 31 (wrapping u64 math). `rnd() real` is `(next() >> 11)` as real divided by 9007199254740992.0, in [0, 1). `range(a real, b real) real` is a plus (b minus a) times rnd(). `vary(v real, frac real) real` is v times (1 plus frac times (2 rnd() minus 1)). `pick(n int) int` is the floor of rnd() times n, at most n minus 1.

`lat(ix int, iy int, iz int) u64` hashes a lattice point with nseed: start from nseed, and for each coordinate (as u64, wrapping) add it times `0x9E3779B97F4A7C15` then apply the splitmix64 mixing steps (without changing `st`). `noise(p double3) real` is improved Perlin gradient noise in about [-1, 1]: lattice cell = floor of each coordinate, fraction f, fade `t*t*t*(t*(t*6-15)+10)` per axis, each of the eight corners picks one of the twelve edge gradients (±1,±1,0), (±1,0,±1), (0,±1,±1) by `lat` modulo 12 and dots it with the offset from that corner; trilinear blend with the faded weights. `fbm(p double3, oct int) real` sums oct octaves of noise with amplitude starting at 1 and halving and frequency starting at 1 and doubling, divided by the sum of the amplitudes. `ridged(p double3, oct int) real` is the same sum but of `(1 - |noise|)` squared per octave, divided by the sum of the amplitudes (so in [0, 1]).

`vert(p double3, n double3, c double3) int` appends a vertex (each component stored as f32) and returns its id. `tri(a int, b int, c int)` appends a triangle. `quad(a int, b int, c int, d int)` appends triangles a b c and a c d. `pos(v int) double3`, `nrm(v int) double3` and `col(v int) double3` read a vertex back as reals. `set_pos(v int, p double3)`, `set_nrm(v int, n double3)` and `set_col(v int, c double3)` overwrite one. `tint(c double3, amount real) double3` multiplies each channel by `vary(1, amount)` and clamps it to [0, 1]. `mixc(a double3, b double3, t real) double3` is the linear blend.

`unit(v double3) double3` normalizes v, giving (0, 1, 0) when its length is below 1e-12. `basis(d double3) double3, double3` returns two unit vectors perpendicular to the unit vector d and to each other (cross d with whichever of the X or Y axis is less parallel to d, normalize, then cross d with that). `rot(v double3, axis double3, ang real) double3` rotates v about the unit axis by ang radians (Rodrigues).

`normals(v0 int, t0 int)` recomputes the normals of vertices v0 to the end from triangles t0 to the end: zero them, add each triangle's unnormalized face normal (cross of its two edges from the first corner, so the length weights by area) to its three corners, then store `unit` of each sum.

`move(v0 int, d double3)` shifts vertices v0 to the end by d. `spin(v0 int, c double3, axis double3, ang real)` rotates their positions about point c and their normals about the unit axis. `scale(v0 int, c double3, s real)` scales their positions about c by s.

`bounds(v0 int) double3, double3` returns the smallest and largest corner of vertices v0 to the end (both zero when there are none). `fit(v0 int, base double3, size real)` scales vertices v0 to the end about base so the largest side of their bounding box equals size (nothing when that side is below 1e-12).

`tube(path, radius, cols, sides, caps)` (path a read-only slice of double3 points, radius a read-only slice of reals, cols a read-only slice of double3 colors, sides an int, caps a bool; returns an int) sweeps a ring of `sides` vertices along the path (all three slices read-only and of one length n, at least 2) and returns the first vertex id. Tangent at point i is `unit` of the next point minus the previous one (one-sided at the ends). The ring frame uses parallel transport: the first normal axis comes from `basis` of the first tangent; at each later point take the previous normal axis, remove its component along the new tangent and normalize it (when that fails use `basis` again); the binormal is the tangent crossed with the normal axis. Ring vertex k at point i is at the path point plus radius[i] times (cos a times the normal axis plus sin a times the binormal), a = PI2 times k / sides, with that unit direction as its normal and cols[i] as its color. Side quads join ring i and ring i plus one so they face outward: corners (i, k), (i, k+1), (i+1, k+1), (i+1, k) with k+1 wrapping to 0. When caps is true add one center vertex at each end (start normal minus the first tangent, end normal the last tangent, colors cols[0] and cols[n-1]) and fan it to the end ring with outward winding, so the tube is closed. Check the winding by the signed volume of a straight capped tube: it must be positive.

`revolve(c, prof, segs, ribs, rib_depth, wobble, cols)` (c a double3, prof a read-only slice of reals, segs and ribs ints, rib_depth and wobble reals, cols a read-only slice of double3 colors; returns an int) turns a profile about the vertical axis through c and returns the first vertex id. prof is read-only pairs (radius, height) from the top of the shape down to its bottom; a pair whose radius is below 1e-9 becomes one pole vertex on the axis instead of a ring. Profile point j at angle a = PI2 times k / segs gets radius `r * (1 - rib_depth * 0.5 * (1 - cos(ribs * a))) * (1 + wobble * noise(double3 { 3 cos a, 3 times its height, 3 sin a }))` and position c plus (radius cos a, height, radius sin a); its color is cols[j] darkened by `1 - 0.35 * rib_depth * 0.5 * (1 - cos(ribs * a))`. Join neighboring rings with quads and poles with fans, all facing outward (a profile that starts and ends on poles gives a closed shape), then `normals` over the new vertices and triangles.

`ico(c double3, r real, levels int) int` builds a unit icosahedron subdivided `levels` times (each triangle into four, midpoints shared through a dictionary keyed by the two parent ids, smaller first, so the mesh stays closed), every vertex pushed out to radius r around c, with the outward direction as its normal and white as its color; returns the first vertex id.

`open_edges(t0 int) int` counts the undirected edges of triangles t0 to the end that are used by a number of triangles other than two (dictionary keyed by `(min << 32) | max`). `volume(t0 int) real` is the signed volume of triangles t0 to the end: the sum of `dot(a, cross(b, c)) / 6` over their corners. `bad_normals(v0 int) int` counts vertices v0 to the end whose normal has a NaN or a length off 1 by more than 1e-3. `digest() u64` is FNV-1a 64 over the raw bits of every value in P, N and C (four bytes each, little end first) and then every value in I (as four little-endian bytes).

`glb_bytes(out)` (out a growable byte array passed by reference) clears out and writes the mesh as a binary glTF 2.0 file. The binary chunk holds, in order, positions (f32 VEC3), normals (f32 VEC3), colors (f32 VEC3) and indices (u32 SCALAR), each part starting on a 4-byte boundary. The JSON chunk declares `asset` version "2.0" with generator "Helium grow", one scene with one node with mesh 0, one mesh with one primitive (attributes POSITION 0, NORMAL 1, COLOR_0 2, indices 3, material 0, mode 4), one material (`pbrMetallicRoughness` with baseColorFactor [1,1,1,1], metallicFactor 0, roughnessFactor 0.87, and `doubleSided` true), one buffer with the binary chunk's length, four bufferViews (targets 34962 for the vertex parts and 34963 for the indices) and four accessors (componentType 5126 for the vertex parts, 5125 for the indices; POSITION gets `min` and `max` from `bounds(0)`, each printed so it reads back as the same f32, like Python's repr of the f32 value). JSON is padded with spaces and the binary chunk with zeros to a multiple of 4. Header: magic `0x46546C67`, version 2, total length; chunk headers: length then type `0x4E4F534A` for JSON and `0x004E4942` for BIN, all u32 little-endian. `glb(path)` (path a read-only byte slice; returns a bool) writes `glb_bytes` to the file at path with `write_file`.
